 <tr>
  <td class="print" align="right">14.05.2005 - 10:55</td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
  <td class="print" align="left"><br />Der <b>Everquest 2 Community Manager  Moorgard</b> hat in den offiziellen Everquest 2-Foren einige Fragen zum dem kontrovers diskutierten „Station Exchange“beantwortet.<br />
<table class="quote" width="98%" cellpadding="4" cellspacing="1" align="center"><tr><td class="quote"><b>Zitat</b><br /><font class="quote"> <b>SOE has always contended that the play style of pro farmers and the secondary market in general has a negative impact on the game, server economies and other players. Does SOE still believe that is true?</b><br />
That's a generalized question, and I prefer to answer it in precise terms. The EQ2 Team is committed to maintaining a safe and secure environment for all our players who abide by the rules of conduct and behavior that we have established. Anyone who breaks those rules, farmer or not, is subject to disciplinary action.<br />
Trust me, I've seen many anti-social jerks in my years of playing MMOs, and I doubt any of them were plat farmers. We target those who make the game un-fun for the good people who play this game, and if those jerks are farmers, it just makes it that much easier to find them and eliminate them.<br />
By the same token, if Joe from Ohio chooses to play on an Exchange-enabled server full-time and sell the right to use the items he earns through legitimate play that doesn't interfere with anyone else, he can do that. Even as someone who has no interest in playing on an Exchange-enabled server myself, I don't feel that someone like Joe hurts my play experience in any way.<br />
 </font></td></tr></table><br /><br />
<a target="_blank" href="http://eqiiforums.station.sony.com/eq2/board/message?board.id=stex&message.id=14067#M14067">Zum Posting von Moorgard</a></td>
 </tr>
